{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_018pBN367wNS9h7VwrcnebK3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. Now I understand the pattern for attack triggers. Let me implement sos_1.\n\nThe card spec:\n- Cost reduction: {1} less per instant/sorcery in your graveyard (generic only, ≥ 0)\n- Reach keyword\n- On attack: may cast target instant/sorcery from graveyard for free\n- If that spell would go to the graveyard, exile it instead\n\nSo:\n1. `cost_reduction` hook: count instant/sorcery in own graveyard\n2. `Keyword.REACH`\n3. `register_triggers`: when this creature attacks, find instant/sorcery in the graveyard and prompt the player to cast one for free, then set up a replacement effect to exile that specific spell instead of sending it to the graveyard\n\n.\n\nFor the replacement effect, I need to register it when the spell is cast and have it intercept the move-to-graveyard event for just that spell, changing the destination to exile. The tricky part is cleanup—since the spell never enters the battlefield, the normal unregister logic won't fire automatically. I'm thinking about whether registering the replacement with the spell as source will handle the cleanup after it resolves and moves to exile.\n\nLooking at how the replacement manager tracks applied replacements within a single event, it prevents duplicates but doesn't auto-unregister between events. So I'll need to either manually unregister the replacement after it fires, or have the replacement function itself call unregister on the spell object once it's done modifying the event. Since the replacement function is called inline and not on the stack, I can safely unregister the source object after modifying the event. The ReplacementManager iterates fresh with `_collect_matching` on each loop, so if I unregister during the replacement function, the next iteration simply won't find that effect anymore—which is exactly what I want.\n\nFor the \"may\" trigger on the attack ability, I'll let the player choose a target instant or sorcery from their graveyard, and if they decline by choosing None, nothing happens. The auto-select suggestion in the TODO seems like a UX enhancement rather than a core requirement, so I'll keep it simple for now.\n\nSince DeterministicPlayer just pops choices from the script, I need to check if there are any legal targets first before prompting. The implementation registers the trigger on the attack event, then lets the player select which instant or sorcery to cast for free, or pass if they don't want to.\n\nThe tricky part is handling the exile requirement: after casting the spell for free from the graveyard, I need to intercept it before it would normally return to the graveyard and send it to exile instead using a replacement effect.\n\nBut I'm realizing there's a problem with how replacements work in the code—they only apply when a permanent is leaving the battlefield, not when a non-permanent spell moves from the stack to the graveyard. So my replacement effect won't actually fire when the spell tries to move to the graveyard after resolving. I need to look more carefully at the resolution logic to find where I can actually intercept this. Looking at how fdn_244 registers its replacement effect, I see it's defining a handler that modifies the event and sets `prevented = True` to stop the default behavior. So the TODO is suggesting I register a similar `ReplacementEffect` for `MoveToGraveyardReplacementEvent` that changes the destination to exile instead of letting it go to the graveyard.\n\nBut I'm realizing there's a deeper issue here—the engine only applies replacements when `leaving_battlefield = True`, and for a spell resolving from the stack to the graveyard, that condition is false. Even if I register the replacement effect, it won't actually fire because `move_to_zone` is called without a `replacement_event` parameter in the spell resolution logic, so the replacement manager never gets invoked in the first place.\n\nThe cleanest solution is to add a check directly in `_resolve_spell` that detects whether the card should be exiled instead of going to the graveyard, and redirect it accordingly. This keeps the logic localized to spell resolution without requiring broader changes to the zone-movement system. Modifying move_to_zone to apply replacements even when not leaving the battlefield, so that instant and sorcery spells can have their graveyard destination replaced before being moved there. Writing replacement event logic... I'm reconsidering whether to use an `on_resolve` hook or make a minimal engine change instead. The `_resolve_spell` function in `engine/casting.py` could be updated to apply replacement effects when moving non-permanents from the Stack to the Graveyard, which would be a justified and localized modification.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1085,"cache_read_input_tokens":104214,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1085},"output_tokens":26,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e9c65f7-ddbe-4353-b4d6-7512c729a9ad","uuid":"c9e32c4c-af39-4fc1-a807-82d9d097288e","request_id":"req_011CbwFraM9WXFvoLsEovkxY"}
