{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01WhE2w5PW2nzjW3Lvye34e8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"There's no `ActivateAbility` class in the test utilities. The TODO mentions it but it doesn't exist in test_utils.py. So for testing the activated abilities, I can directly call `ability.cost(game, source)` and `ability.effect(game)`.\n\nActually, looking at how abilities.py works:\n\n```python\ndef _activate_regular_ability(game, player, ability: ActivatedAbilityInstance):\n    cost_paid = ability.cost(game, ability.source)\n    if not cost_paid:\n        raise AbilityError(...)\n    if ability.is_mana_ability:\n        ability.effect(game)\n    else:\n        # Push to stack\n        game.stack.push(...)\n```\n\nSo to test directly, I can get the card's activated abilities, create an `ActivatedAbilityInstance` from the ability, and call `activate_ability(game, player, instance)`. Or I could just invoke `ability.cost(game, source)` and `ability.effect(game)` directly, though that bypasses the real engine path. For the land tests, I think testing through the engine by directly activating is the way to go.\n\nLooking at how to construct an `ActivatedAbilityInstance`, I need to pass the source, controller, cost, effect, and whether it's a mana ability. But I'm realizing that mana abilities from `get_mana_abilities()` are `ManaAbility` instances with `cost` and `mana_produced` properties, and I need to figure out how `activate_ability` handles these different ability types.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":280,"cache_read_input_tokens":165936,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":280},"output_tokens":33,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e9c65f7-ddbe-435