{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01DBhfhQ7yqjLoogShpEPBTN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So `copy_spell` does a shallow copy of the source card and calls `on_resolve` on it. The copy's `on_resolve` calls `copied_card.on_resolve(g)`. \n\nFor Paradigm, I need to distinguish between the original card's first resolution and subsequent copy resolutions. Looking at the Paradigm mechanic:\n\n- \"After you first resolve a spell with this name\" - this is a one-time trigger\n- Once the first resolution happens, the card is exiled\n- The recurring trigger fires at each of your precombat main phases\n\nFor the implementation:\n- When `on_resolve` is called, check if this is a \"copy\" (shallow copy from `copy_spell`) vs the original card\n- I could track a flag on the card itself to mark whether Paradigm has already been set up\n- The original card gets exiled after first resolution, and when the copy resolves later, it skips the Paradigm setup since it's not the original\n- But I need a way to distinguish the original from the copy without modifying `copy_spell` — maybe checking if the card is still in the game state versus being exiled\n\nActually, rethinking this: the Paradigm mechanic itself handles the distinction naturally. The original resolves, exiles itself, and registers the recurring trigger. Then each precombat main phase, a copy gets cast from exile. When that copy resolves, it does its own library exile and free cast, but there's no need to set up Paradigm again since the original already did that.\n\nSo I could add an `_is_paradigm_copy` flag when creating the copy for the recurring trigger, then check for that flag in `on_resolve` to skip the Paradigm setup on copies.\n\nBut wait—I need to think about whether Casualty copies would also trigger the Paradigm setup. Since those copies are created externally, they wouldn't have my flag, so they'd try to set up Paradigm again. Actually, maybe I should use a `_paradigm_setup` flag instead to track whether the setup has already happened, which would be cleaner for handling all copy scenarios. Shallow copies create new objects with the same attributes, so if `_paradigm_active` starts as False, the copy would also try to set up Paradigm when it resolves. The better approach is to track this at the game level using a registry keyed by card name — once \"Improvisation Capstone\" has triggered its paradigm, subsequent copies won't trigger it again. I can use `getattr(game, \"paradigm_activated\", None)` to avoid modifying the game state directly. The key insight is that copies created by the Paradigm trigger are explicitly generated by that mechanism, so I can mark them distinctly—either by tracking which card instances came from the trigger, or by checking if the card was created as a copy rather than cast from a normal zone. This way, when the copy resolves, it can skip the exile and re-trigger setup since it's already part of the recurring loop.\n\nI'll use a simple attribute check with getattr to determine whether this resolution should set up Paradigm or not, keeping the logic straightforward for this specific implementation.\n\nWriting the resolution handler... I'm realizing the timing here is crucial—when `on_resolve` gets called, the card has already been popped off the stack, so I can't just move it from the stack to exile. Instead, I need to let the normal flow continue to the graveyard and then intercept it there with a replacement effect, similar to how the other card handles exile-instead logic.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":819,"cache_read_input_tokens":83994,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":819},"output_tokens":34,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null