{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01AsRu8GEGurx1x73DxukHsz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I now have a clear picture. Let me implement sos_120.\n\nThe card has two main behaviors:\n1. **Main effect**: Exile cards from top of library until total CMC >= 4. May cast any non-land for free.\n2. **Paradigm**: After first resolution, exile this card. At each of your precombat main phases, may cast a copy of it from exile for free.\n\nImplementation plan:\n```python\nclass ImprovisationCapstone(Sorcery):\n```\n\nWriting the main effect...\n```\n\n```\n\npython\nresolve_spell(g, card, player, stack_obj),\n)\ngame.stack.push(stack_obj)\n```\n\nSo the `on_resolve` callback of the StackObject is actually a lambda that invokes `_resolve_spell`. When the test pops and resolves the stack object, it triggers this sequence: the lambda calls `_resolve_spell`, which sets the card's targets, calls `card.on_resolve(game)` where I register the exile replacement, then applies the replacement effects that redirect the card to exile. This matches the same flow as the sos_1 pattern.\n\nNow looking at `_resolve_top_of_stack` in test_utils, it pops objects from the stack and directly calls their `on_resolve` method. When a spell was cast via `cast_spell`, that `on_resolve` is the lambda wrapping `_resolve_spell`, so the test execution follows the same path as the actual game flow.\n\nFor `cast_spell_free`, I need to check what `on_resolve` gets attached to its StackObject.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1268,"cache_read_input_tokens":93394,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1268},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e9c65f7-ddbe-4353-b4d6-7512c7